'use strict';
const {build,ref,NODE}=require('./fullstack-wave3-helper');
const units=[
{comp:'m1.c1',title:'Validate the network boundary beyond shared types',before:'Complete React foundations and Node/Express. Review JSON, TypeScript erasure and server-side validation.',intro:'A React dashboard and its API may import the same TypeScript interface while a stale browser still receives an incompatible JSON response. Shared types catch mistakes in checked source; runtime validation checks bytes that actually cross the network. Both belong to the contract.',sections:[['Specify the wire representation','Define status, content type, required fields, nullability and error shapes as part of an API contract. JavaScript Date objects serialize as strings and undefined object properties disappear from JSON. A shared in-memory type must account for the actual wire form rather than assume runtime objects arrive intact.','Treat incoming JSON as unknown until decoded. A TypeScript assertion such as as Invoice does not validate data. A boundary decoder should reject missing fields and incorrect types, then produce a safe domain value. Client validation protects presentation assumptions; server validation still protects writes.'],['Give incompatible data an explicit path','Do not silently turn a malformed balance into zero and display a successful financial result. Distinguish transport failure, invalid response and valid empty state. Record a safe correlation id and show a recoverable error appropriate to the user action.','The pure decoder below checks a synthetic response shape. It does not execute React rendering, HTTP or a schema library. A real integration fixture should exercise the shared contract against both producer and consumer, including old supported client versions and error payloads.']],problem:'Decode a valid invoice and reject a numeric string.',steps:['Check object and field types.','Construct a safe projected value.','Exercise valid and invalid wire data.'],code:'function decode(x){if(!x||typeof x.id!=="string"||!Number.isFinite(x.total))throw Error("contract error");return {id:x.id,total:x.total};}\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(decode({id:"i1",total:42,internal:true})));try{decode({id:"i1",total:"42"});}catch(e){console.log(e.message);}',expect:'{"id":"i1","total":42}\ncontract error',result:'The decoder rejects incompatible runtime data and projects public fields. Shared source types alone would not perform this check.',mistakes:['A type assertion does not validate JSON.','A malformed response must not masquerade as a valid zero result.'],takeaways:['Specify wire types, not only in-memory interfaces.','Decode untrusted responses before using domain assumptions.'],practice:[
'What does a TypeScript assertion do to received JSON at runtime?|No validation|Automatic schema decoding|Database authorization|Network decryption|Type assertions are erased and do not inspect incoming values.',
'How is a Date commonly represented in JSON?|A string|A live Date instance|A database connection|An executable method|The wire form must be decoded if domain logic expects a Date object.',
'A response total is the string 42 under a numeric contract. What should the decoder do?|Reject the mismatch|Assume the server is correct|Use truthiness as proof|Treat it as a missing invoice|A numeric contract needs an explicit numeric check.',
'Which additional contract dimension matters besides field names?|Nullability and statuses|Only folder naming|The developer font|The repository star count|Consumers need a complete representation and outcome contract.'],formal:[
'What does a shared TypeScript interface primarily check?|Checked source compatibility|Received bytes automatically|Database permission|Network reachability|Source typing does not replace runtime boundary validation.',
'What should received JSON be treated as before decoding?|Unknown input|Trusted domain state|An approved transaction|A rendered component|Network data must be checked before assuming its shape.',
'An object field is undefined before JSON serialization. What may happen?|The property is omitted|It becomes a function|It always becomes false|It retains the undefined keyword|JSON object serialization omits undefined-valued properties.',
'A mobile client receives a number where a timestamp string is required. Which layer should detect this?|The response decoder|Only the CSS layout|The package lock|The database pool maximum|Runtime decoding checks the actual wire representation.',
'A billing UI replaces invalid totals with zero and labels the page successful. What is wrong?|Contract failure is hidden|Zero proves the server had no data|The decoder guarantees correctness|The network request must have failed|Malformed financial data should not become a plausible successful value.',
'An API returns a new error shape but the client reads only success fields. What contract coverage is missing?|Failure payload behavior|Only text capitalization|Only the request hostname|The number of route modules|Supported outcomes include error representations as well as successful data.',
'A developer removes server validation because React validates the form. What boundary is lost?|Arbitrary-client checks|Only frontend type inference|Only response caching|A source-code optimization|Callers can bypass the browser and send invalid commands directly.',
'A decoder checks total with typeof number but accepts NaN from an internal object fixture. What stronger rule helps?|A finite-number check|A larger string length|A second type assertion|A route rename|A numeric domain contract may need finiteness beyond the number type.',
'A producer and consumer both compile but a contract fixture fails at runtime. What should reviewers inspect?|Actual wire data|Only import ordering|Only build cache speed|The number of test files|Compilation cannot prove that emitted JSON matches consumer assumptions.',
'A response includes internal audit fields beside public data. What decoder behavior stabilizes the client boundary?|Explicit public projection|Blind object spreading|Persisting every unknown field|Disabling shape checks|Projection limits the domain object to the contract the client uses.']},
{comp:'m1.c2',title:'Evolve contracts while old clients remain active',before:'Review runtime decoding and distinguish additive changes from changes that alter existing meaning.',intro:'A cached web bundle can remain open while a new API version deploys. Backward compatibility therefore depends on the oldest supported consumer, not only the source currently on main. Plan contract changes as a sequence with observed adoption and an explicit retirement policy.',sections:[['Classify the semantic change','Adding an optional response field may be compatible for clients that ignore unknown fields, but a strict decoder can reject it. Making a required request field mandatory breaks callers that omit it. Changing units from cents to dollars while keeping the same number type is a semantic break even when shared types compile.','Use explicit API versions or a carefully managed compatibility policy when behavior cannot remain compatible. A version path alone does not make a migration safe; both versions need supported semantics, tests and a retirement plan. Keep error shapes and enum expansion in the compatibility review.'],['Use an expand-and-contract sequence','First add a compatible field or endpoint while continuing the old contract. Update consumers to support the new representation, observe usage, then retire the old form only after the agreed support window. During overlap, avoid two conflicting definitions of the same business value.','The model below accepts a legacy cents field or the newer explicit amount object and normalizes both to integer cents. It rejects an ambiguous object containing both representations. This is a small adapter policy, not a live rollout or a guarantee that all currency arithmetic is safe.']],problem:'Normalize two supported contract versions without ambiguous precedence.',steps:['Identify which versioned representation is present.','Reject conflicting representations.','Normalize each valid form to cents.'],code:'function cents(x){const old=Object.hasOwn(x,"cents"),fresh=Object.hasOwn(x,"amount");if(old===fresh)throw Error("ambiguous");return old?x.cents:x.amount.value;}\nconsole.log(cents({cents:1200}),cents({amount:{currency:"USD",value:1200}}));try{cents({cents:1,amount:{value:2}});}catch(e){console.log(e.message);}',expect:'1200 1200\nambiguous',result:'Both declared representations normalize under a bounded policy. Production code additionally needs currency, range and type validation.',mistakes:['A field with unchanged type can still change meaning incompatibly.','A new version name does not define retirement policy.'],takeaways:['Test supported old consumers during change.','Expand compatibility before contracting the legacy representation.'],practice:[
'Which change can break callers despite unchanged numeric types?|Changing cents to dollars|Adding a source comment|Renaming a private variable|Reordering an internal helper|Units are part of semantic compatibility.',
'What does an expand-and-contract sequence begin with?|Compatible additional support|Immediate deletion of old fields|Forced data loss|Disabling all tests|Consumers need overlap before the old contract is retired.',
'Can an optional added response field break a strict decoder?|Yes, with strict decoding|No, optional always means safe|Only if the field is numeric|Only after a database restart|Compatibility depends on actual consumer decoding policy.',
'Why test the oldest supported client?|It may still be active|It always has the newest types|It owns server authorization|It prevents all incidents|Deployed or cached clients can outlive an API source change.',
'A required request property is added without a default. What happens to old callers that omit it?|They may fail validation|They automatically generate it|They gain authorization|They always receive a cache hit|Making new input mandatory can break existing requests.',
'What should accompany endpoint retirement?|Support-window evidence|Only a new folder name|Only a package comment|A silent error rename|Retirement needs an explicit compatibility and adoption policy.',
'An enum gains a new value. What client behavior needs testing?|Exhaustive handling and fallback|Only CSS imports|Only dependency installation|The number of HTTP sockets|Consumers may assume the earlier enum was closed.',
'Why reject conflicting old and new amount fields in the model?|Avoid ambiguous precedence|Improve database indexing|Make every request fast|Authenticate the caller|Two disagreeing representations need a deliberate conflict policy.'],formal:[
'What is backward compatibility relative to?|Supported existing consumers|Only current main-branch source|Only the latest server binary|Every imaginable future program|Compatibility is assessed against the consumers the service promises to support.',
'What does an API version identifier fail to supply by itself?|Contract lifecycle policy|A recognizable label|A distinguishable URL token|A routing choice|Version naming does not manage the lifecycle of supported contracts.',
'A browser tab opened yesterday calls today’s API. Which assumption is unsafe?|All clients are current|Old clients may remain active|Runtime validation is useful|Overlap can be necessary|Web deployment does not instantly replace every running consumer.',
'A response changes price 1200 from cents to dollars without a field rename. What kind of break is this?|A semantic contract change|Only a formatting change|A guaranteed safe additive change|A Git metadata change|The meaning of the number changed even though its type did not.',
'A new required request field rejects old requests. Which transition could preserve compatibility?|Compatible default or API|Deleting validation entirely|Returning 200 for invalid writes|Removing the old client tests|A deliberate compatibility mechanism is needed during migration.',
'A strict client rejects all unknown JSON properties. What must an additive field rollout account for?|That actual decoder policy|Only field spelling|Only compression level|The number of API servers|An additive server change can still violate a strict consumer contract.',
'A legacy endpoint has no observed traffic for one hour. What additional context matters before retirement?|Agreed support window|Only the newest build duration|Only the route filename|The number of log colors|Short observation alone may miss infrequent supported consumers.',
'An amount adapter accepts old and new fields together and chooses one silently. What risk appears?|Conflicting representations are hidden|The currency is automatically correct|All callers are migrated|The database is necessarily corrupt|Ambiguous inputs need a deliberate precedence or rejection policy.',
'A team adds a new enum outcome and all old clients use an exhaustive switch. What should the compatibility test examine?|Unknown-outcome behavior|Only JSON whitespace|Only Git commit length|Only SQL connection reuse|Enum expansion can expose assumptions that every possible value was known.',
'After consumers migrate, why keep a documented contract retirement step?|Manage legacy retirement|Guarantee all future upgrades|Eliminate every test|Replace authorization|Compatibility has a managed lifecycle rather than an indefinite unnoticed overlap.']},
{comp:'m1.c1',title:'Define mutation commands and stable error contracts',before:'Use contract decoders and understand HTTP outcomes. Commands and queries have different retry consequences.',intro:'An expense approval command should carry the intended change and a concurrency precondition rather than a copy of every displayed field. A response contract tells the UI which value became authoritative and how known conflicts differ from internal or transport failures.',sections:[['Design a narrow command','Separate read models from writable command fields. A read response may include calculated totals or privileged audit fields that must never be posted back as editable input. Define required preconditions such as expectedVersion when concurrent edits matter. Validate the command on the server even if a shared client type exists.','Return the committed representation or a stable result resource so the client can reconcile its view. A 204 response intentionally has no content to decode; a client should not always call response.json. A non-2xx fetch response still resolves its promise and requires an explicit status check.'],['Map outcomes into usable client states','Use stable machine-readable error codes for known conflicts or validation failures, with safe messages for display. The client should distinguish rejected command, uncertain transport outcome and successful commit. Do not infer transaction rollback merely because response parsing failed.','The model classifies synthetic response descriptors without making HTTP requests. It shows that a no-content success is distinct from a conflict and an internal failure. Real tests must verify the producer emits the declared statuses and that the client handles malformed or missing bodies.']],problem:'Classify mutation response outcomes before attempting body decoding.',steps:['Check success and no-content status.','Recognize a known conflict status.','Keep unknown server failures separate.'],code:'function classify(status){if(status===204)return "success:no-content";if(status>=200&&status<300)return "success:decode";if(status===409)return "conflict";return "failure";}\nconsole.log([204,200,409,500].map(classify).join(","));',expect:'success:no-content,success:decode,conflict,failure',result:'Status classification guides the next decode step. It does not prove a command committed or reconcile an uncertain network failure.',mistakes:['fetch does not reject solely because the HTTP status is 500.','A 204 response must not be treated as a required JSON body.'],takeaways:['Use narrow commands with explicit preconditions.','Classify status and content before decoding a mutation result.'],practice:[
'A successful 204 mutation response has what body contract?|No content|Required JSON invoice|A raw database record|A mandatory stack trace|The client must not require a JSON body for a declared no-content response.',
'Why separate read models from command fields?|Some fields are read-only|Every read is a transaction|Commands need no validation|It removes authorization|Calculated or privileged fields must not become client-controlled writes.',
'How does fetch normally handle an HTTP 500 response?|A resolved HTTP response|It always throws a network error|It retries forever|It changes the status to 200|HTTP failure status requires explicit checking of the response.',
'What can expectedVersion express in an edit command?|A write precondition|A browser font|A production secret|The SQL driver release|The server can reject writes based on stale state.'],formal:[
'What is a machine-readable error code useful for?|Stable outcome handling|Replacing all explanations|Encrypting the response|Authorizing every retry|A stable code supports consistent branching independently of display wording.',
'What is an API command model?|The allowed intended write input|Every field read from storage|A browser screenshot|The entire database schema|Commands define permitted changes rather than mirror all stored data.',
'An edit form posts ownerId and approvalStatus copied from a read model. What should the server enforce?|A narrow command schema|Trust the displayed values|Write every property|Authorize by field count|Read-visible fields are not automatically client-writable.',
'A client calls json on every response and fails after a successful 204. What contract branch is missing?|No-content success|Automatic transaction rollback|Database retry|A required error stack|The absence of a body is intentional for the declared status.',
'A client treats every resolved fetch as business success. Which missing check matters?|HTTP status and result contract|Only Promise type|Only request URL length|The component file extension|Fetch can resolve for unsuccessful HTTP statuses.',
'A server commits an edit but its response body is malformed. What can the UI safely conclude?|Invalid observed result|The write certainly rolled back|The user certainly lacks access|No request reached the server|Response decoding failure does not prove the server effect outcome.',
'A user submits an old version while another edit has committed. Which declared outcome can preserve the precondition?|A conflict response|Silent overwrite always|A fake successful body|An unrelated login redirect|A controlled conflict lets the client reconcile stale state.',
'An error message is localized. What should client logic generally branch on?|A stable error code|Exact translated sentence|The punctuation count|The stack line number|Display text changes independently of the machine contract.',
'A mutation response returns authoritative updated data. What should the UI avoid?|Finalizing stale optimism|Reconciling to the server result|Validating the result shape|Clearing the pending marker|The committed representation supersedes provisional client assumptions.',
'A request fails before any response arrives. How should its outcome differ from a known 409?|Transport uncertainty is a separate state|Both prove no side effect universally|Both are successful writes|Neither needs user feedback|A known rejection and a missing observation carry different evidence.']},
{comp:'m2.c1',title:'Reconcile optimistic mutations with server authority',before:'Review React immutable updates, mutation commands and concurrency preconditions.',intro:'A task board can display a moved card immediately while the server checks the command. That optimistic value is provisional. The UI needs a pending state, authoritative reconciliation and a failure policy that cannot erase a newer user action.',sections:[['Identify server state and local intent','Server state is an observed representation owned by the API; local state includes draft text, focus and a pending intent. Copying server data into unrelated component state without a synchronization policy creates multiple competing truths. Use stable resource ids and record which mutation produced each provisional view.','On success, reconcile to the returned committed representation or invalidate and refetch relevant queries. An invalidation marks data stale; it does not guarantee a fresh response has already arrived. Show pending or stale state intentionally while reconciliation completes.'],['Guard rollback against overlapping writes','A simple snapshot rollback works only if no later mutation has superseded the provisional state. Tag each optimistic change with a token or maintain a mutation log. A failed older request must not restore a snapshot over a newer successful edit. Server-side version checks handle actual write conflicts.','The model keeps a pending token and ignores an older failure after a newer result has committed. It demonstrates a client-state guard rather than a complete cache library. Integration tests should combine overlapping responses, server conflicts and navigation to prove the actual UI behavior.']],problem:'Prevent a failed old optimistic mutation from overwriting a newer result.',steps:['Apply two mutations with distinct tokens.','Commit the newer result.','Ignore rollback from the older token.'],code:'let state={value:"old",token:0};function optimistic(value,token){state={value,token};}function fail(token,before){if(state.token===token)state={value:before,token};}optimistic("first",1);optimistic("second",2);state={value:"server second",token:2};fail(1,"old");console.log(state.value);',expect:'server second',result:'The older failure cannot restore the stale snapshot. Actual server ordering and concurrency remain separate requirements.',mistakes:['Invalidation is not immediate freshness.','An unconditional snapshot rollback can erase newer work.'],takeaways:['Mark optimistic values as provisional.','Reconcile and roll back using mutation identity.'],practice:[
'What is optimistic UI data before server confirmation?|Provisional state|A guaranteed committed write|A database constraint|A permanent audit record|Optimistic presentation anticipates a result that can still be rejected.',
'What should a successful authoritative mutation result normally replace?|The provisional value|Every unrelated local draft|The entire browser history|All query keys|The server result reconciles the resource whose write completed.',
'Does cache invalidation mean a fresh response is already displayed?|No, it marks stale data|Yes, always synchronously|Only if the id is numeric|Only for POST requests|Fresh retrieval and application still need to complete.',
'An older failed mutation rolls back over a newer success. What guard is needed?|Mutation identity|A longer label|A second uncontrolled retry|Only a new component file|Rollback must apply only to the provisional state it owns.'],formal:[
'Who owns the authoritative result of a committed server mutation?|The server contract|The optimistic snapshot|A component variable alone|The loading spinner|A provisional client assumption must reconcile with the committed result.',
'What is local intent state in an edit form?|An unsaved draft|A guaranteed database value|A successful API response|A shared server transaction|Draft input can exist independently of the last server observation.',
'A mutation succeeds and the API normalizes whitespace. What should the UI display after reconciliation?|The returned normalized value|The stale optimistic text forever|A fabricated failure|Only an empty state|Authoritative normalization is part of the committed result.',
'An older failed request arrives after a newer edit succeeded. What rollback policy is safest?|Check mutation ownership|Always restore the oldest snapshot|Clear every cached resource|Retry both forever|A rollback must not overwrite state owned by a later mutation.',
'A query is invalidated after an edit. What state may still be visible while refetch runs?|Pending stale data|Guaranteed fresh data|A committed rollback|A new database schema|Invalidation and completed fresh retrieval are separate events.',
'Two components copy one server row into independent mutable state. What maintenance risk appears?|Competing stale copies|Automatic shared authority|Guaranteed atomic updates|No reconciliation needed|Multiple unmanaged copies can diverge after mutations.',
'A user edits an amount while the server calculates tax. Which value should the final total use?|The committed server result|Only the typed estimate|The first cached total forever|A random optimistic amount|The authoritative calculation supersedes a provisional estimate.',
'A failed optimistic delete restored a list item at the wrong position. What contract should the rollback preserve?|Owned state and ordering|Only the item title|Only the HTTP method|The package version|Rollback must restore the relevant representation without trampling later changes.',
'A pending save remains visible after navigation. What should state design identify?|Mutation and resource identity|Only component color|Only the route string length|Only request body size|Pending work must be associated with the resource and command it represents.',
'Two optimistic edits overlap, the first fails, and the second succeeds with a server version. What combined policy preserves both UI and write correctness?|Token guard and version check|Always restore initial data|Only duplicate local snapshots|Use response arrival as write order|Client mutation identity protects rollback while server preconditions govern actual concurrent writes.']},
{comp:'m2.c2',title:'Build complete query keys and stable pagination',before:'Know filters, sorting and cursor-based APIs. A cache key identifies a representation, not just a route.',intro:'A support portal caches ticket pages for several tenants and filters. If its key contains only tickets, a filtered page or another user’s data can appear in the wrong view. Query identity must include every input that changes the response and the relevant authorization scope.',sections:[['Define representation identity','Include resource, tenant or principal scope, filters, sort order, page or cursor and any contract version that changes the representation. Normalize equivalent inputs deliberately so object-key order does not create accidental duplicates. Avoid placing raw credentials in keys or logs.','Offset pagination is simple but inserts can shift rows between requests. Cursor pagination needs a stable ordering with a unique tie-breaker, such as created_at plus id. A cursor represents continuation under an API policy, not universal snapshot consistency. Concurrent changes still require documented semantics.'],['Coordinate filters and real-time updates','Changing a filter should reset a cursor that belongs to the old result set. Apply real-time events only if they belong to the active scope and are newer under the declared version scheme. Gaps or reconnects may require a refetch rather than assuming every event was delivered.','The key builder demonstrates deterministic scope and filter identity using a fixed tuple. It does not implement a cache library or cursor cryptography. A production cache also needs lifecycle, retention and logout rules; private cached data must not be reused under a different identity.']],problem:'Build distinct keys for separate tenant and filter representations.',steps:['Use an ordered tuple of response-changing inputs.','Serialize the tuple consistently.','Compare equivalent and distinct requests.'],code:'const key=(tenant,status,cursor)=>JSON.stringify(["tickets",tenant,status,cursor??null]);console.log(key("a","open",null)===key("a","open",undefined));console.log(key("a","open",null)===key("b","open",null));console.log(key("a","open",null)===key("a","closed",null));',expect:'true\nfalse\nfalse',result:'Equivalent empty cursors share a key while tenant and filter changes do not. This fixture does not authorize either tenant.',mistakes:['A URL-only key may omit tenant and filter scope.','A cursor from one filter must not be reused blindly for another.'],takeaways:['Include all representation-changing inputs in query identity.','Use stable ordering and document concurrent pagination semantics.'],practice:[
'What must a private query key account for?|Identity scope|Only the route name|Only response length|Only a component id|Private representations may differ by authorized principal or tenant.',
'Why reset a cursor when a filter changes?|It has a different scope|Every cursor is a password|Filters cannot be cached|It commits a transaction|Continuation tokens are tied to the query policy they represent.',
'Which order supports stable cursor tie-breaking?|created_at then unique id|Only a nonunique status|Random order per call|Only the current row count|A unique tie-breaker distinguishes rows with equal sort values.',
'Can a cursor alone guarantee a consistent snapshot under all writes?|No, semantics must be defined|Yes, any cursor freezes storage|Only if it is base64|Only in JavaScript|Continuation and snapshot guarantees are separate contracts.',
'A ticket key omits status=open. What bug can follow?|Filtered views collide|The server must crash|Authentication becomes stronger|Every query becomes fresh|Different representations can incorrectly share a cache entry.',
'A websocket reconnects after missing events. What may be necessary?|Refetch or gap recovery|Assume no state changed|Replay no checks ever|Trust arrival order universally|Delivery gaps require an explicit reconciliation mechanism.',
'Why not include a raw access token in a query key?|It can leak through tooling|It makes all keys unique safely|It prevents expiry|It replaces authorization|Keys can appear in logs and development diagnostics.',
'A user signs out and another signs in. What must private cache lifecycle consider?|Clearing or rescoping data|Only changing the page title|Only reloading CSS|Keeping every old private result|Identity transitions must preserve private-data isolation.'],formal:[
'What does a query cache key identify?|A specific representation|Every user universally|A database connection|A browser tab only|The key must distinguish inputs that change the returned view.',
'What is a cursor used for in pagination?|Continuation under a policy|Guaranteed write rollback|User authentication by itself|An unlimited query budget|A cursor identifies how to continue a defined ordered result set.',
'A cache key contains only /tickets while tenant and status vary. What is missing?|Representation scope|Only timestamp formatting|Only HTTP compression|The npm script name|Scope and filters can change the response at the same path.',
'Rows share the same created_at timestamp. What should cursor ordering include?|A unique tie-breaker|Only the shared timestamp|A random order each page|Only display text|A total stable order needs a way to distinguish tied rows.',
'New records appear before page two in offset pagination. What effect is possible?|Duplicates or skipped rows|Guaranteed snapshot isolation|Automatic cursor repair|No result change ever|Offsets can shift as records are inserted or removed.',
'A user changes from open to closed tickets but keeps the old cursor. What should the client do?|Reset continuation state|Assume the cursor is universal|Disable all authorization|Append results without checking|A continuation belongs to the earlier filtered result set.',
'A private cache survives account switching under one shared key. What failure is possible?|Cross-account data exposure|Automatic stronger isolation|Only a sorting difference|A guaranteed server outage|Client cache identity and cleanup must match authorization scope.',
'A real-time event has an older resource version than the cached row. What policy can prevent regression?|Ignore stale versions|Always apply last arrival|Reset every row to zero|Trust network speed as order|Delivery arrival does not always match resource-state order.',
'Equivalent filters produce different object-property orders in key serialization. What should be normalized?|Canonical key inputs|Only the response label|The database password|The user role itself|Equivalent requests should map consistently without accidental serialization differences.',
'A cursor feed reconnects after missing events while filters also changed. Which recovery plan is coherent?|Rescope key and refetch|Apply all old events blindly|Reuse the old cursor forever|Treat reconnect as full freshness|The active representation and delivery gap both require deliberate reconciliation.']},
{comp:'m2.c1',title:'Invalidate related views without erasing local drafts',before:'Review optimistic state, query keys and authoritative mutation responses.',intro:'A project rename can affect a detail view, several filtered lists and a navigation label. Updating one visible component does not synchronize every cached representation. Define the dependency between a mutation and the views it changes, while preserving unrelated unsaved input.',sections:[['Choose update or invalidation by available evidence','If a mutation returns a complete authoritative row, update the detail cache directly when its representation matches. A filtered list may still need refetch because the new row could enter or leave that result set. Do not blindly append an item to every page or every filter.','Invalidate the smallest relevant family of keys under the correct scope. Broad invalidation is simpler but can create request bursts and discard useful data; overly narrow invalidation leaves stale representations. Document the tradeoff and test the views that reflect one mutation.'],['Protect draft state during background refresh','A background read should not overwrite a user’s unsaved draft merely because a fresh server row arrived. Keep the draft distinct, track its base version and show a conflict or refresh decision when the authoritative resource changes. Navigation and successful save can reset it under explicit rules.','The fixture replaces only server-owned cache data and leaves a draft unchanged. It models a separation of responsibilities, not a React hook implementation. Actual UI tests should verify controlled inputs, background refresh, conflict messages and final successful reconciliation.']],problem:'Apply fresh server state while preserving a separate unsaved draft.',steps:['Keep cache and draft as separate values.','Replace the observed server representation.','Inspect both values after refresh.'],code:'let cache={id:"p1",name:"old",version:1};const draft={name:"unsaved",baseVersion:1};cache={id:"p1",name:"remote",version:2};console.log(cache.name,draft.name,draft.baseVersion!==cache.version);',expect:'remote unsaved true',result:'The fresh server observation does not silently overwrite draft input. The version difference provides evidence for an explicit conflict policy.',mistakes:['Updating one detail key does not refresh every filtered list.','A background refetch should not silently destroy unsaved text.'],takeaways:['Map mutations to their affected representations.','Separate drafts from server observations and track their base version.'],practice:[
'A renamed project may leave a filtered list. What cache action may be needed?|Refetch the affected list|Append it to every list|Ignore list filters|Clear every local draft|Membership can change, so a detail row update alone may be insufficient.',
'What should a background refresh preserve in an edit form?|Unsaved draft intent|Only the old server row|A stale authorization token|Every optimistic assumption forever|Draft state needs a lifecycle separate from server cache observations.',
'Why store a draft base version?|Detect remote changes|Guarantee automatic merging|Avoid every validation check|Replace user identity|The base version identifies which server state the draft was based on.',
'What is a risk of invalidating all queries after every keystroke?|Unnecessary request bursts|Guaranteed atomic saving|Stronger authorization|No network traffic|Broad repeated invalidation can cause excessive refetch and UI churn.'],formal:[
'What can a complete authoritative mutation response update directly?|Matching detail cache|Every arbitrary filtered page|All unsaved forms|The whole database schema|Direct cache replacement needs the corresponding complete representation.',
'What does a draft base version record?|The observed edit starting point|The current server truth forever|The browser language|The database password|A draft can become stale relative to later authoritative versions.',
'A category change means an item no longer belongs in a cached filtered list. What should reconciliation account for?|List membership|Only the item id|Only response indentation|The Git branch|A mutation can change both row fields and representation membership.',
'A save refresh overwrites unrelated unsaved form text. What state design is missing?|Separate draft ownership|A larger HTTP timeout|A smaller query key|A new package name|Server cache refresh and local user intent require independent lifecycles.',
'A mutation updates one tenant but invalidates all tenants. What tradeoff is poorly scoped?|Excessive refetch demand|Guaranteed cross-tenant authorization|Automatic stronger consistency|A necessary SQL rollback|Invalidation should generally follow the affected representation and scope.',
'A list has several cursor pages and the edited row changes sort position. What simple update is unsafe?|Appending the row everywhere|Refetching affected pages|Preserving query scope|Checking stable resource ids|Blind insertion can duplicate or misorder a row across pages.',
'A draft was based on version 3 while refresh shows version 4. What should the UI offer under a conflict policy?|An explicit reconcile decision|Silent overwrite always|Fake success|Automatic deletion of history|A changed base needs a deliberate merge, refresh or conflict path.',
'A mutation response contains only an acknowledgement, not the new row. What is often required for fresh display?|Refetch authoritative data|Invent missing fields|Treat the old snapshot as new|Skip reconciliation forever|An acknowledgement may not supply enough data to reconstruct the updated representation.',
'A background refresh is pending after invalidation. What should the status communicate?|Freshness is still pending|All writes rolled back|Every view is already current|The user is signed out|The UI should not equate a refresh request with its completed result.',
'One mutation changes row fields, filter membership and draft base version. What reconciliation plan handles the combined effect?|Separate view policies|Overwrite all state from one response|Append row to every page|Clear all user input immediately|Each representation has different ownership and completeness; one blind replacement cannot safely satisfy all three.']},
{comp:'m3.c1',title:'Render partial success and recoverable failure explicitly',before:'Review fetch status handling, local drafts and mutation uncertainty.',intro:'An account page may load profile data while its optional activity panel fails. Treating every failure as a blank page discards useful work, while labeling a failed save successful misleads the user. Model each operation’s outcome and the business importance of its missing data.',sections:[['Use states that preserve meaning','Distinguish initial loading, loaded empty data, loaded content, background refresh and failure. A failed request is not an empty list. Preserve a successful independent panel when an optional panel fails, and display safe retry controls for the failed boundary. Critical dependent data may require blocking the action instead.','Promise.all rejects when one input rejects, but the other operations may still continue. Promise.allSettled can collect independent outcomes when partial success is allowed. It is not a reason to ignore required failures or claim a multi-step business transaction was atomic.'],['Make recovery understandable','Keep entered form data after a rejected save and associate errors with the relevant fields or operation. For unknown mutation outcomes, present checking or reconciliation rather than a blind retry that may duplicate effects. Use stable command identities when the server supports idempotent recovery.','The example combines an independent successful profile with an optional failed panel. It logs result states rather than rendering React. A real journey test should verify accessible status messages, focus behavior, retry scope and that successful content survives the failed panel.']],problem:'Collect independent panel outcomes without losing successful data.',steps:['Start two synthetic independent operations.','Collect fulfilled and rejected results.','Keep the successful payload and label the failed panel.'],code:'(async()=>{const results=await Promise.allSettled([Promise.resolve({name:"Ada"}),Promise.reject(Error("activity unavailable"))]);console.log(results[0].status,results[0].value.name);console.log(results[1].status);})();',expect:'fulfilled Ada\nrejected',result:'Independent partial success is preserved. No multi-step transaction or React rendering is demonstrated.',mistakes:['A failed request is not a valid empty result.','allSettled does not create transaction atomicity.'],takeaways:['Represent partial outcomes at the correct operation boundary.','Preserve useful content and user input during recovery.'],practice:[
'An activity request fails. Is an empty activity list an equivalent state?|No, failure is unknown data|Yes, both prove no activity|Only if the name is short|Only with HTTP GET|An empty result is known data while failure does not establish content.',
'What can allSettled preserve for independent panel requests?|Each operation outcome|Atomic database writes|Guaranteed retry success|A shared transaction|Independent outcomes can support an intentional partial-success UI.',
'A form save is rejected by validation. What should usually be preserved?|The entered draft|Only the loading spinner|A fake success message|An empty form|Keeping input lets the user correct the relevant problem.',
'An optional panel fails while profile data loads. What can remain visible?|The successful profile|Only a blank page always|A false full-success claim|Raw server secrets|Partial rendering can preserve useful independent content.'],formal:[
'What distinguishes an empty result from a failed read?|Absence versus unknown|Only response color|Only elapsed time|Nothing meaningful|Failure does not prove that the underlying collection is empty.',
'What does Promise.allSettled return for rejected inputs?|Their rejected outcomes|A committed transaction|Only successful values|Automatic retries|The result preserves per-operation settlement rather than fail-fast aggregation.',
'A dashboard hides every successful panel when optional news fails. What policy could improve resilience?|Independent panel states|Claiming all news succeeded|Retrying the whole page forever|Clearing every cache key|Independent noncritical outcomes can be rendered separately.',
'A profile save fails and the form clears. What recovery property was lost?|Preserved user input|Database isolation|A package lock|API route matching|The user should not have to reconstruct a valid draft after a rejected operation.',
'A checkout requires inventory and payment success. Can allSettled alone make the business operation atomic?|No, coordinate the business effect|Yes, every settled set is atomic|Only if both promises are async|Only if errors are logged|Outcome collection does not provide transaction or compensation semantics.',
'A response timeout follows a possible write. Which retry UI needs care?|A blind repeat command|A safe status check|Preserving input|A bounded reconciliation step|Unknown effects can be duplicated if an unguarded retry creates a new logical command.',
'A background refresh fails while cached data is available. What can the UI disclose?|Disclosed stale data|Guaranteed freshness|A confirmed empty database|A successful save|The previous observation may remain useful if its stale status is clear.',
'A critical price lookup fails but purchase remains enabled with a fabricated price. What invariant is violated?|Required purchase inputs|Only page aesthetics|Only text localization|The source module count|Critical actions must not proceed on invented required data.',
'A failed panel retry clears a separate successful panel. What boundary should be narrower?|Recovery scope|The database schema|The server process id|The CSS import|Recovery should target the failed operation without discarding unrelated success.',
'An account page has successful profile data, a failed optional panel and a save with unknown effect. Which model preserves all three meanings?|Independent typed outcomes|One global success boolean|An empty array for all failures|A forced full-page reload loop|Different operation contracts require distinct states and recovery actions rather than one page-wide flag.']},
{comp:'m3.c2',title:'Trace a defect across browser, API and storage',before:'Know request identifiers, safe logging and repository boundaries. Logs are evidence with a limited scope.',intro:'A student sees a stale enrollment count after saving successfully. The browser, API and database may each have different observations. Trace the logical operation using a safe id and timestamps, then test the boundary where the evidence first diverges from the contract.',sections:[['Connect observations without leaking payloads','Propagate a bounded request or trace identifier through browser diagnostics, API logs and database adapter events where appropriate. Record operation category, resource identity under policy, duration and outcome. Avoid raw tokens, passwords and unnecessary private fields. A trace id correlates work; it is not an authorization credential.','Separate network timing from server timing and UI application time. A successful API response followed by a stale component can indicate cache or rendering behavior. A wrong persisted value points to server rules or storage. Adjacent log lines are not necessarily the same request under concurrency.'],['Build a reproducible seam test','Capture a synthetic failing sequence with the relevant versions, filter inputs and mutation result. Verify request payload, API response, stored state and cache key at their own boundaries. Use the smallest real integration seam that can reproduce the mismatch rather than mocking the suspected defect away.','The fixture groups interleaved synthetic events by id to show a request’s path. It cannot establish distributed clock order or database commit visibility. A real trace also requires authorized diagnostic access and careful interpretation of retries and asynchronous jobs.']],problem:'Extract one request path from interleaved safe diagnostic events.',steps:['Give each event a correlation id.','Select events for the target operation.','Inspect layer and outcome sequence.'],code:'const events=[{id:"r1",layer:"browser",outcome:"send"},{id:"r2",layer:"api",outcome:"other"},{id:"r1",layer:"api",outcome:"200"},{id:"r1",layer:"cache",outcome:"stale"}];console.log(events.filter(e=>e.id==="r1").map(e=>e.layer+":"+e.outcome).join(","));',expect:'browser:send,api:200,cache:stale',result:'Correlation isolates a synthetic path and suggests the cache seam deserves inspection. It does not prove the real root cause.',mistakes:['A trace id is not permission to access a resource.','Adjacent concurrent log lines do not prove one causal path.'],takeaways:['Follow one operation across layer-specific observations.','Reproduce the first contract mismatch at its real boundary.'],practice:[
'What does a request id primarily provide?|Correlation|Authorization|Encryption|Transaction locking|An id connects related observations without granting resource access.',
'API returns correct new data but the component displays old data. Which seam deserves inspection?|Client cache and rendering|Only SQL syntax|Only deployment region|Only the TLS certificate|The mismatch appears after the API observation rather than in the returned value.',
'Why avoid logging raw access tokens during debugging?|They are reusable credentials|They cannot be copied|They prove database integrity|They improve every trace|Diagnostic convenience does not justify credential exposure.',
'Are adjacent server log lines necessarily one request?|No; requests interleave|Yes, always|Only on a quiet day|Only for GET routes|Concurrent operations can produce neighboring unrelated events.',
'What should a reproducible defect fixture use?|Synthetic controlled data|A private customer dump|Only a screenshot|A random production account|Controlled synthetic inputs make the sequence repeatable without unnecessary private data.',
'A mocked adapter removes the suspected storage defect. What is needed?|A real adapter fixture|More identical mocks|A larger component file|A longer status label|A substitute cannot establish behavior at the boundary it replaces.',
'What can distributed timestamps fail to prove by themselves?|Exact causal order|That a timestamp was recorded|The displayed time string|The field name|Clock skew and async work limit inference from timestamps alone.',
'What should tracing record besides duration?|Operation and outcome|Every password|All raw cookies|The complete environment|Safe categorized outcomes make timing evidence interpretable.'],formal:[
'What does a cross-layer trace connect?|Related operation observations|All authorization decisions automatically|Every database row|Unrelated source files|Correlation helps follow one logical operation through multiple boundaries.',
'What is a useful minimal defect report input?|Synthetic reproduction|A production password|Only an emotional label|The entire private database|Controlled steps and expected versus actual behavior support investigation.',
'A browser sends the expected payload but the API stores the wrong amount. Where should evidence next focus?|Server/storage seam|Only client CSS|Only the browser font|Only query-cache age|The persisted mismatch appears beyond the correct request boundary.',
'The API and database are correct but one filtered list remains stale. What should the trace include?|Query key and invalidation|Only database credentials|Only package authors|The number of servers|Cache representation identity and refresh behavior are relevant to the observed client mismatch.',
'Interleaved logs show an error next to another request’s success. What prevents false grouping?|Correlation identifiers|Only text proximity|Only matching durations|Only shared route names|Distinct requests can interleave even on the same route.',
'A trace logs all cookies to identify requests. What should be changed?|Use safe bounded context|Publish the logs|Base64 the cookies|Disable every request id|Cookies can contain credentials and are unnecessary for basic correlation.',
'A test uses a fake for the exact adapter suspected of corrupting data. What is the evidence gap?|The defect seam was replaced|The fake proves production behavior|The database must be correct|Every component was exercised|The relevant real adapter must execute in an isolated reproduction.',
'A request schedules a background job and returns before it finishes. What should tracing distinguish?|Request and job lifetimes|Only log color|Only URL length|The user’s font size|Asynchronous work can continue beyond the request response and needs its own linked observations.',
'Two machines have slightly different clocks. What should reviewers avoid assuming?|Time proves causality|Each event has an id|Durations can be useful|Clock policy matters|Cross-host timestamp comparisons need clock and causality context.',
'A stale UI incident includes retries, a successful commit and delayed events on another host. Which evidence plan best isolates the fault?|Linked attempts and real seams|Only the last adjacent log line|Only another full-page screenshot|Only a fake repository success|Attempt identity, effect observations and cache application must be correlated without treating arrival order as universal causality.']},
{comp:'m3.c1',title:'Reject stale responses and recover uncertain saves',before:'Review independent failures, query scope and mutation identity. Request completion order can differ from user intent order.',intro:'A search request for an earlier filter can finish after a newer search and overwrite the intended result. Likewise, an aborted browser request may still have caused a server write. Use explicit identity guards for reads and a different recovery contract for uncertain mutations.',sections:[['Guard application of read results','Capture a monotonically increasing request token or representation key for each read. Apply a response only if it still belongs to the active request and scope. AbortController can reduce unnecessary browser work, but a completion guard still expresses the state ownership rule clearly.','React effects should clean up subscriptions and pending work when dependencies change. Do not rely on effect timing to provide server-side authorization or ordering. The active query key and the currently authenticated scope both need to match before private results are applied.'],['Treat save uncertainty as an effect problem','Aborting fetch stops the browser’s participation according to its API behavior; it does not universally roll back a server mutation. For writes, use a stable logical command key where supported, a status resource or a reconciliation read. Preserve user input until the application has a justified final outcome.','The fixture applies only the latest read result despite out-of-order completion. It is a deterministic reducer model, not an actual React effect or AbortController test. A full UI fixture should also verify component unmount, account changes and a save whose response is lost after commit.']],problem:'Ignore an old result after a new query became active.',steps:['Set the active request token.','Attempt to apply a newer response.','Deliver an older response and preserve the current value.'],code:'let active=2,view="loading";function apply(token,value){if(token===active)view=value;}apply(2,"new filter");apply(1,"old filter");console.log(view);',expect:'new filter',result:'The active token prevents the stale read from overwriting the current view. Server effects are not modeled or cancelled.',mistakes:['Response arrival order need not match user intent order.','Aborting a client request does not universally undo a write.'],takeaways:['Apply reads only to their active representation.','Recover uncertain writes using effect identity and authoritative evidence.'],practice:[
'Two searches complete out of order. What should choose the displayed result?|Active request identity|Last network arrival always|Largest response body|Oldest request always|The view belongs to the currently intended query, not arbitrary completion order.',
'What can a response token guard prevent?|Stale read overwrite|Every server-side write race|Database corruption universally|All HTTP failures|A client guard controls whether a result may update its current view.',
'Does aborting fetch guarantee a server write rolled back?|No; reconcile the effect|Yes, always|Only for JSON requests|Only with a long timeout|Client cancellation does not universally cancel or undo server-side work.',
'Why clean up subscriptions when an effect’s scope changes?|Stop obsolete updates|Authorize all future data|Guarantee zero memory use|Replace server validation|Old subscriptions can continue delivering data for an inactive view.'],formal:[
'What ordering should a latest-search UI preserve?|Current user intent|Every response arrival|Alphabetical request ids|Largest body first|The active query defines which result belongs in the view.',
'What does a read completion guard check?|Result ownership of active state|Database transaction isolation|The password format|The npm package license|Only a result matching the current request or representation should apply.',
'A slow old filter response arrives after a fast new one. What action avoids regression?|Ignore the obsolete result|Overwrite the new result|Append both blindly|Reset the account id|Completion order need not match the active search intent.',
'A component switches tenants while an old private request is pending. What must application of its result check?|Current identity scope|Only response size|Only status phrase|The old component title|Private data must not enter a view under a different active authorization scope.',
'A browser aborts a save after the API committed. What should recovery use?|Authoritative reconciliation|A guaranteed no-effect claim|A new unrelated write key|Only an empty UI|The effect may exist even though the client stopped observing the response.',
'A search effect subscribes again without cleaning up the old stream. What can happen?|Obsolete updates continue|Automatic deduplication always|A durable transaction|All streams stop|Effect lifecycle cleanup prevents inactive subscriptions from mutating current views.',
'A client retries a save using a new command id after every timeout. What risk appears?|Duplicate logical effects|Guaranteed replay protection|Automatic conflict resolution|No request reaches the API|Stable command identity is needed when retries represent the same intended write.',
'A response guard uses only a numeric token but account changes reset the counter. What else should be scoped?|The authenticated identity|Only response indentation|Only the component filename|The route font|Tokens must be interpreted within the current identity and representation context.',
'A result is rejected as obsolete. Does that prove its server query never ran?|No; execution can occur|Yes, the guard cancels storage|Only if the result is JSON|Only if the request was fast|A client-side application guard does not control all upstream execution.',
'An account switch overlaps an old read and a save with lost response. Which combined recovery rule is sound?|Scope reads; reconcile writes|Drop all guards and retry|Treat abort as rollback|Apply last arrival globally|Read result ownership and uncertain write effects need distinct policies that preserve identity isolation and business correctness.']}
];
units.forEach((u,i)=>{u.difficulties=i<5?['E','E','M','M','M','M','H','H','H','A']:['E','E','M','M','M','H','H','H','H','A'];});
